This study demonstrates a logical consequence of optimal control being a strong solution to the 1-D heat equation
\begin{equation*}
  y_t = (k(y_{xx})_x) + z \text{ in } (0,T) \times (0,1),
\end{equation*}
along with a dynamic boundary condition
\begin{equation*}
  y \big|_{x=0,1} = 0,
\end{equation*}
by the same method used by Russell and Zhang in establishing a logarithmic convexity property for the 1-D heat equation.
As a result, strong primitive optimality principles become applicable, and impulsive controllability is achievable using a Carleman estimate-based method.
